theory:
## Safe Type Conversions

```xiom
pub interface From[Src] {
  fn from(value: Src) -> Self;
}

pub interface Into[Tgt] {
  fn into(self) -> Tgt;
}
```

### Implementing From

```xiom
pub type UserId = Int;

impl From[Int] for UserId {
  fn from(id: Int) -> UserId {
    // Could add validation:
    // requires: id > 0;
    return id;
  }
}

fn main() {
  let uid: UserId = UserId::from(42);  // Explicit
  let uid2: UserId = 42.into();        // Implicit via Into
}
```

### From Str to Custom Type

```xiom
pub type Email = Str;

impl From[Str] for Email {
  fn from(s: Str) -> Email {
    // Validation could go here
    return s;
  }
}

fn subscribe(email: &Email) { /* ... */ }

subscribe(&"user@example.com".into());  // Str → Email conversion
```

### Auto-Into from From

Implementing `From` automatically gives you `Into` — you never need to implement both. The standard library provides a blanket impl.

### Chaining Conversions

```xiom
let path: PathBuf = "data/config.toml".into();  // Str → PathBuf
let file = File::open(&path)?;  // PathBuf accepted
```

### Multiple From Impls

```xiom
impl From[Int] for UserId { fn from(id: Int) -> UserId { return id; } }
impl From[Str] for UserId { fn from(s: Str) -> UserId { return s.parse_int().unwrap(); } }
```

